INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_30',
  'What is a primary benefit of using Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) with a large language model?',
  'mcq',
  '["Eliminating the need for any prompt engineering","Reducing the model''s knowledge cutoff","Grounding responses in specific, up-to-date data","Significantly reducing the cost of LLM inference"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'A primary benefit of using Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) (Option C) is grounding LLM responses in specific, up-to-date, and authoritative data, which helps reduce hallucinations and provides more relevant answers. RAG does not eliminate the need for prompt engineering (Option A), nor does it directly reduce the model''s inherent knowledge cutoff (Option B) but rather augments it with external data. While it can improve accuracy, it doesn''t inherently significantly reduce the cost of LLM inference (Option D); costs might even increase due to retrieval operations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_31',
  'Which generative AI use case involves condensing long documents or articles into shorter, coherent versions while retaining key information?',
  'mcq',
  '["Code generation","Image generation","Text summarization","Sentiment analysis"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Text summarization (Option C) is a generative AI use case that involves condensing long documents or articles into shorter, coherent versions while retaining key information. Code generation (Option A) creates programming code. Image generation (Option B) creates visual content. Sentiment analysis (Option D) determines the emotional tone of text.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_32',
  'A company needs an LLM to answer questions based on its proprietary internal documents. The responses must be accurate and up-to-date. They want to achieve this with the LEAST effort and cost. Which approach is generally most suitable?',
  'mcq',
  '["Fine-tuning a foundation model with all internal documents","Prompt engineering alone","Implementing Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)","Training a new LLM from scratch"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Implementing Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) (Option C) is generally the most suitable approach for grounding an LLM in proprietary internal documents with the least effort and cost. RAG retrieves relevant information from a knowledge base and provides it to the LLM, ensuring responses are accurate and up-to-date without expensive model retraining. Fine-tuning a foundation model (Option A) is expensive and time-consuming, requiring curated training data, and is often overkill when RAG suffices. Prompt engineering alone (Option B) would not provide the LLM with access to the specific proprietary knowledge. Training a new LLM from scratch (Option D) is extremely costly and complex, far exceeding the ''least effort and cost'' constraint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_33',
  'What limitation of generative AI models refers to the fact that their knowledge is limited to the data they were trained on, up to a certain point in time?',
  'mcq',
  '["Hallucinations","Context limits","Knowledge cutoff","Cost"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'The knowledge cutoff (Option C) refers to the limitation of generative AI models where their knowledge is restricted to the data they were trained on, up to a specific point in time. This means they cannot access or generate information about events or data that occurred after their training data was collected. Hallucinations (Option A) are incorrect but plausible outputs. Context limits (Option B) refer to the maximum input/output length. Cost (Option D) is a practical limitation but not directly related to the temporal aspect of knowledge.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_34',
  'In the context of generative AI, what is the primary purpose of "agents and tool use"?',
  'mcq',
  '["To fine-tune foundation models","To enable LLMs to perform multi-step tasks by interacting with external systems","To reduce the context window size of LLMs","To pre-train new foundation models"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'The primary purpose of "agents and tool use" (Option B) in generative AI is to enable LLMs to perform complex, multi-step tasks by interacting with external systems, APIs, or databases (tools). This allows them to go beyond simple text generation and perform actions in the real world. It is not for fine-tuning models (Option A), reducing context window size (Option C), or pre-training new models (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_35',
  'A software development team wants to automate the creation of boilerplate code and suggest code snippets based on natural language descriptions. Which generative AI use case is most relevant?',
  'mcq',
  '["Sentiment analysis","Summarization","Code generation","Translation"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Code generation (Option C) is the most relevant generative AI use case for automating the creation of boilerplate code and suggesting snippets from natural language descriptions. Sentiment analysis (Option A) determines emotional tone. Summarization (Option B) condenses text. Translation (Option D) converts text between languages.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_36',
  'A company wants to deploy an internal chatbot for employees to ask questions about company policies. They are considering using a base foundation model directly. What is the main limitation of this approach?',
  'mcq',
  '["The base model will be too expensive for internal use","The base model will only provide generic answers and lack company-specific knowledge","The base model requires extensive fine-tuning for basic Q&A","The base model cannot handle natural language queries"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'The main limitation of using a base foundation model directly for an internal company chatbot (Option B) is that it will only provide generic answers and lack company-specific knowledge, as its training data has a knowledge cutoff and no access to proprietary internal documents. While cost is a factor (Option A), the primary functional limitation here is the lack of specific, internal knowledge. Base models are generally capable of basic Q&A (Option C) and handling natural language queries (Option D) without extensive fine-tuning for general tasks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_37',
  'Which approach involves updating a pre-trained foundation model''s weights with a smaller, domain-specific dataset to adapt it to a particular task or style?',
  'mcq',
  '["Prompt engineering","Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)","Fine-tuning","Zero-shot inference"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Fine-tuning (Option C) involves updating a pre-trained foundation model''s weights with a smaller, domain-specific dataset to adapt it to a particular task or style. Prompt engineering (Option A) involves crafting effective prompts without changing model weights. Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) (Option B) augments prompts with retrieved information. Zero-shot inference (Option D) is a type of prompt engineering where no examples are given.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_38',
  'What limitation of generative AI models makes it difficult to understand exactly why a model produced a particular output, often referred to as a "black box" problem?',
  'mcq',
  '["High cost","Context window limits","Lack of interpretability","Data privacy concerns"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Lack of interpretability (Option C) is a significant limitation of generative AI models, making it difficult to understand the exact reasoning or internal mechanisms behind a model''s specific output, leading to the "black box" problem. While high cost (Option A), context window limits (Option B), and data privacy concerns (Option D) are also limitations, they do not directly describe the difficulty in understanding the model''s decision-making process.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_39',
  'A global e-commerce company needs to automatically convert product descriptions from English to several other languages for its international websites. Which generative AI use case is most appropriate?',
  'mcq',
  '["Content generation","Sentiment analysis","Machine translation","Summarization"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Machine translation (Option C) is the most appropriate generative AI use case for automatically converting text, such as product descriptions, from one language to another. Content generation (Option A) is broader and could include creating new descriptions, but translation specifically addresses language conversion. Sentiment analysis (Option B) determines emotional tone, and summarization (Option D) condenses text.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_40',
  'What is the primary advantage of implementing Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) for an LLM-powered application that needs to stay current with rapidly evolving internal company policies?',
  'mcq',
  '["RAG allows for fine-tuning the LLM with new data more frequently.","RAG reduces the computational cost of LLM inference.","RAG allows for updating the knowledge base without retraining the LLM.","RAG eliminates the possibility of LLM hallucinations."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the primary advantage. RAG enables LLMs to access and incorporate the latest information from an external knowledge base (like internal company policies) without requiring expensive and time-consuming retraining (fine-tuning) of the LLM itself. This makes it highly agile for dynamic data. Option A is incorrect; RAG is an alternative to frequent fine-tuning. Option B is incorrect; while RAG can improve efficiency by focusing the LLM, its primary benefit isn''t necessarily reducing inference cost. Option D is incorrect; RAG significantly reduces hallucinations by grounding responses, but it does not entirely eliminate them.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_41',
  'A user asks an LLM about a major global event that occurred last month, but the LLM responds that it does not have information on that topic. This is a clear example of which inherent limitation of LLMs?',
  'mcq',
  '["Hallucination.","Context window limit.","Knowledge cutoff.","Nondeterminism."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is knowledge cutoff. Large Language Models are trained on vast datasets collected up to a certain point in time. Information or events that occur after this training cutoff date are not part of the model''s knowledge base, leading to its inability to answer questions about them. Option A, Hallucination, is generating incorrect but plausible information. Option B, Context window limit, refers to the maximum input/output length. Option D, Nondeterminism, refers to varied outputs for the same input.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_42',
  'Which generative AI concept allows an LLM to go beyond text generation by autonomously calling external APIs or tools to retrieve real-time information or perform actions on behalf of the user?',
  'mcq',
  '["Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG).","Fine-tuning.","Agents and tool use.","Prompt engineering."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Agents and tool use. This advanced capability allows an LLM to act as an ''agent'' that can reason, plan, and execute actions by interacting with external systems, databases, or APIs (tools). This enables it to perform tasks like fetching real-time data, sending emails, or updating records, going beyond just generating text. Option A, RAG, focuses on retrieving information for grounding responses. Option B, Fine-tuning, adapts the model''s behavior or knowledge. Option D, Prompt engineering, guides the model''s output through instructions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_43',
  'What is a primary benefit of using Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) in generative AI applications?',
  'mcq',
  '["It allows LLMs to access and generate responses based on up-to-date, proprietary, or domain-specific information beyond their training data.","It completely eliminates the need for any prompt engineering, making LLMs easier to use out-of-the-box.","It reduces the computational cost of LLM inference to near zero, making all applications highly cost-effective.","It enables LLMs to perform complex mathematical calculations with perfect accuracy without external tools."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (It allows LLMs to access and generate responses based on up-to-date, proprietary, or domain-specific information beyond their training data) is the primary benefit of RAG. RAG addresses the knowledge cutoff and lack of domain-specific knowledge in base LLMs by retrieving relevant information from external, authoritative sources and incorporating it into the LLM''s response. Option B (It completely eliminates the need for any prompt engineering) is incorrect; prompt engineering is still crucial for effective RAG. Option C (It reduces the computational cost of LLM inference to near zero) is incorrect; RAG adds retrieval costs and still incurs LLM inference costs. Option D (It enables LLMs to perform complex mathematical calculations with perfect accuracy) is not a direct benefit of RAG; LLMs often require external tools or agents for accurate calculations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_44',
  'A company wants to deploy an internal chatbot for employees to ask questions about company policies and internal documents. They decide to use a base foundation model directly, without any additional mechanisms to incorporate their proprietary knowledge. What is the most significant limitation of this approach?',
  'mcq',
  '["The base model will be too expensive to operate for internal use cases.","The chatbot will provide generic answers and may hallucinate information, lacking company-specific context.","The base model will require extensive fine-tuning for every new policy update, increasing operational overhead.","The chatbot will only be able to answer questions about public knowledge, not internal documents."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (The chatbot will provide generic answers and may hallucinate information, lacking company-specific context) is the most significant limitation. Base foundation models have a knowledge cutoff and are trained on public data, meaning they have no access to proprietary company policies or internal documents. Without grounding mechanisms like Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) or fine-tuning, the chatbot will generate generic, potentially incorrect, or hallucinated responses when asked about internal knowledge, which is an anti-pattern (ap-2.2-002). Option A (The base model will be too expensive) is a potential concern but not the primary functional limitation regarding knowledge. Option C (The base model will require extensive fine-tuning for every new policy update) is incorrect; fine-tuning is generally for adapting the model''s style or specific tasks, and RAG is typically preferred for dynamic knowledge updates. Option D (The chatbot will only be able to answer questions about public knowledge) is partially true but doesn''t fully capture the risk of hallucination or generic answers.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_45',
  'Which of the following is a known limitation of generative AI models?',
  'mcq',
  '["Their inability to process any form of natural language input.","Their perfect accuracy in all factual tasks without external validation.","Their tendency to ''hallucinate'' or generate plausible but incorrect information.","Their requirement for constant human supervision for every output generated."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Their tendency to ''hallucinate'' or generate plausible but incorrect information) is a well-known limitation of generative AI models, particularly LLMs. They can produce outputs that sound convincing but are factually wrong or nonsensical. Option A (Their inability to process any form of natural language input) is incorrect; processing natural language is their core capability. Option B (Their perfect accuracy in all factual tasks) is incorrect; LLMs are not perfectly accurate and often require validation, especially for high-stakes tasks. Option D (Their requirement for constant human supervision for every output generated) is an overstatement; while human review is often recommended for critical applications, it''s not required for every single output.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_46',
  'In the context of generative AI, what is the primary role of ''agents'' and ''tool use''?',
  'mcq',
  '["To enable LLMs to perform multi-step tasks by interacting with external systems and APIs.","To fine-tune foundation models with domain-specific data for improved accuracy.","To reduce the computational cost of LLM inference by optimizing model parameters.","To provide a graphical user interface for interacting with generative AI models."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (To enable LLMs to perform multi-step tasks by interacting with external systems and APIs) correctly describes the primary role of agents and tool use. Agents allow LLMs to reason, plan, and execute actions by calling external tools (APIs, databases, custom functions) to gather information, perform calculations, or interact with other systems, thereby extending their capabilities beyond pure text generation. Option B (To fine-tune foundation models) is a model adaptation technique, not related to agents and tools. Option C (To reduce computational cost) is related to model optimization, not agentic workflows. Option D (To provide a graphical user interface) describes a front-end component, not the core function of agents and tool use.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_47',
  'When considering how to adapt a foundation model for a specific business use case, which approach involves providing external, up-to-date information at inference time to ground the model''s responses, rather than altering the model''s weights?',
  'mcq',
  '["Fine-tuning the foundation model with new data.","Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG).","Pre-training a new foundation model.","Developing a custom model from scratch."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)) is the correct answer. RAG involves retrieving relevant information from an external knowledge base (e.g., vectorized documents) at inference time and providing it to the LLM as part of the prompt. This grounds the model''s responses in up-to-date, domain-specific information without modifying the model''s internal weights. Option A (Fine-tuning the foundation model) involves further training the model on a specific dataset to adapt its weights and behavior. Option C (Pre-training a new foundation model) is a much more extensive and resource-intensive process of training a model from the ground up. Option D (Developing a custom model from scratch) is the most complex and time-consuming option, involving designing and training a new model architecture.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

