INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_45',
  'A company has deployed a new customer service chatbot powered by an Amazon Bedrock foundation model. They want to ensure the chatbot''s responses are helpful, accurate, and do not contain any inappropriate language. Which evaluation method is crucial for assessing these subjective qualities comprehensively?',
  'mcq',
  '["Relying solely on ROUGE scores for text similarity.","Conducting A/B testing to compare user engagement with different model versions.","Human evaluation of model outputs for quality, safety, and relevance.","Automated BLEU scores to measure n-gram overlap with reference answers."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) is crucial for assessing subjective qualities like helpfulness, accuracy, and appropriateness. Automated metrics like ROUGE (Option A) and BLEU (Option D) only measure surface-level similarity and do not capture semantic quality, factual accuracy, or safety (ap-3.1-002). A/B testing (Option B) measures user preference or engagement but not the intrinsic quality or safety of the content itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_46',
  'What is the main purpose of Amazon Bedrock Guardrails in a foundation model application?',
  'mcq',
  '["To fine-tune foundation models with proprietary data for specific tasks.","To evaluate the accuracy and performance of different foundation models.","To manage model deployments and versioning in production environments.","To enforce content policies and filter harmful or off-topic outputs from foundation models."]'::json,
  '{3}'::int[],
  'Option D (correct) accurately describes the main purpose of Amazon Bedrock Guardrails. They provide a layer of protection to ensure that model outputs adhere to content policies, preventing the generation of harmful, biased, or off-topic content (ap-3.4-001). Option A describes fine-tuning. Option B describes Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation. Option C describes deployment management, not guardrails.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_47',
  'A social media platform is integrating an Amazon Bedrock foundation model to automatically generate post summaries. They are concerned about the model potentially generating biased or offensive content. Which Amazon Bedrock feature should they implement to proactively filter such outputs?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Bedrock Guardrails.","Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation.","Amazon SageMaker Clarify for bias detection.","A/B testing for user preference."]'::json,
  '{0}'::int[],
  'Option A (correct) is the most direct solution. Amazon Bedrock Guardrails provide configurable content filters, PII redaction, and topic denial lists to enforce safe and responsible usage, proactively filtering harmful or biased content (ap-3.4-001). Option B is for assessing model performance, not real-time filtering. Option C is for detecting bias in training data or model predictions, not for real-time content moderation of LLM outputs. Option D is for comparing user experience, not for content safety.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_48',
  'What does the ''groundedness'' evaluation dimension assess in a foundation model''s output, especially when used with RAG?',
  'mcq',
  '["The model''s ability to generate creative and imaginative text.","The model''s speed of response and inference latency.","Whether the model''s response is supported by the provided source material.","The model''s adherence to a specific output format, such as JSON."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) defines groundedness as assessing whether the model''s output is factually supported by the reference or source material provided, which is crucial for applications using RAG. Option A relates to creativity (temperature parameter). Option B relates to performance. Option D relates to output formatting.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_49',
  'A development team wants to compare two different foundation models for a text summarization task based on their factual accuracy and relevance to the original document. They need a systematic way to assess this before full deployment. Which Amazon Bedrock feature can assist with this comparison?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Bedrock Guardrails for content filtering.","Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases for data retrieval.","Amazon SageMaker for general machine learning model training.","Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation."]'::json,
  '{3}'::int[],
  'Option D (correct) is specifically designed for this purpose. Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation provides tools and features to systematically compare and evaluate different foundation models against specific criteria, including accuracy and relevance, using both automated metrics and human review. Option A is for content moderation. Option B is for RAG. Option C is a broader ML platform, not specific to Bedrock model comparison.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
