INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_3_17',
  'What is the primary goal of ''fine-tuning'' a pre-trained foundation model?',
  'mcq',
  '["To train a model from scratch on a massive, diverse dataset.","To adapt a pre-trained model to perform well on a specific downstream task or domain.","To reduce the number of parameters in the model for faster inference.","To generate new, synthetic data for further pre-training."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes fine-tuning: taking a general-purpose pre-trained model and adapting it to excel at a more specific task or domain using a smaller, task-specific dataset. Option A describes pre-training. Option C is not the primary goal; fine-tuning can sometimes increase parameters or keep them similar. Option D is not the purpose of fine-tuning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_3_18',
  'Which technique allows for efficient fine-tuning of large models by only updating a small subset of parameters?',
  'mcq',
  '["Full model pre-training","Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT)","Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF)","Zero-shot prompting"]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) refers to Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) techniques, such as LoRA or QLoRA, which significantly reduce the computational cost and memory footprint of fine-tuning large models by only updating a small fraction of the model''s parameters. Option A is the opposite, training the entire model. Option C is a method for aligning models with human preferences. Option D is a prompting technique, not a fine-tuning method.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_3_19',
  'What is the main difference between ''pre-training'' and ''fine-tuning'' a foundation model?',
  'mcq',
  '["Pre-training uses a small, task-specific dataset, while fine-tuning uses a large, general dataset.","Pre-training focuses on learning general language patterns, while fine-tuning adapts the model to a specific task.","Pre-training is done with human feedback, while fine-tuning is fully automated.","Pre-training occurs after fine-tuning in the model development lifecycle."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) highlights the core distinction. Pre-training involves training on vast, diverse datasets to learn broad patterns and knowledge. Fine-tuning then takes this pre-trained model and further trains it on a smaller, specific dataset to optimize performance for a particular task or domain. Option A reverses the dataset sizes. Option C is incorrect; pre-training is typically unsupervised, and fine-tuning can involve human feedback (RLHF). Option D is incorrect; pre-training always precedes fine-tuning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_3_20',
  'What does ''instruction tuning'' primarily aim to achieve during the fine-tuning process?',
  'mcq',
  '["To make the model more resistant to prompt injection attacks.","To enable the model to follow human instructions more effectively and generalize to new tasks.","To reduce the model''s memory footprint during inference.","To increase the model''s knowledge base with new factual information."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) describes instruction tuning. It involves fine-tuning a model on a dataset of instructions and corresponding responses, making the model better at understanding and following diverse instructions, and improving its ability to generalize to unseen tasks. Option A is related to security, not instruction following. Option C is related to efficiency, not instruction following. Option D is more aligned with RAG or continued pre-training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_3_21',
  'When is ''Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF)'' typically applied in the training process of foundation models?',
  'mcq',
  '["During the initial pre-training phase on vast datasets.","As a post-fine-tuning step to align the model with human preferences and values.","To generate the initial set of training data for the model.","To convert a text-based model into a multi-modal model."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) accurately places RLHF as a crucial step typically performed after initial fine-tuning. RLHF uses human preferences to train a reward model, which then guides the foundation model to generate outputs that are more helpful, harmless, and aligned with human values. Option A describes pre-training. Option C is incorrect; RLHF uses human feedback on generated outputs, not for generating initial training data. Option D is unrelated to RLHF.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_3_22',
  'A data science team wants to adapt an Amazon Bedrock foundation model to perform exceptionally well on a very specific, niche domain task using a relatively small, high-quality dataset. Which customization approach is most suitable?',
  'mcq',
  '["Relying solely on zero-shot prompting.","Fine-tuning the model with the domain-specific dataset.","Pre-training a new foundation model from scratch.","Always choosing the largest available model for the task."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is the most suitable approach. Fine-tuning allows a pre-trained Amazon Bedrock model to specialize in a niche domain by training it on a high-quality, task-specific dataset, leading to significant performance improvements for that particular task. Option A (zero-shot prompting) might not achieve the ''exceptionally well'' performance required for a niche task. Option C (pre-training from scratch) is extremely resource-intensive and unnecessary when a suitable foundation model already exists. Option D (largest model) is an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001) and not necessarily the best for domain adaptation with a specific dataset.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_3_23',
  'What is the primary benefit of Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) techniques like LoRA?',
  'mcq',
  '["They allow for training foundation models from scratch with less data.","They significantly reduce the computational resources and storage needed for fine-tuning large models.","They eliminate the need for any human feedback in the fine-tuning process.","They guarantee that the fine-tuned model will be completely unbiased."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is the primary benefit of PEFT techniques like LoRA. By only updating a small number of additional parameters or layers, PEFT drastically reduces the memory footprint, computational cost, and storage requirements for fine-tuning large foundation models, making it more accessible. Option A is incorrect; PEFT is for fine-tuning, not training from scratch. Option C is incorrect; human feedback can still be crucial for alignment. Option D is an unrealistic guarantee; PEFT does not inherently remove all bias.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_3_24',
  'What is the consequence of fine-tuning a foundation model on a small, low-quality dataset?',
  'mcq',
  '["It guarantees significant performance improvement on the target task.","It will always result in a model that is faster and more cost-effective.","It can lead to overfitting, amplify existing biases, or degrade model performance.","It is a recommended practice for achieving broad generalization."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) describes the negative consequences of fine-tuning with poor data (anti-pattern ap-3.3-001). Small or low-quality datasets can cause the model to overfit to the noise in the data, amplify existing biases, or simply fail to improve, potentially even degrading its performance. Option A is incorrect; it''s likely to degrade performance. Option B is not a guaranteed outcome. Option D is incorrect; it''s an anti-pattern for generalization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_3_25',
  'A developer needs to update a foundation model to better understand and respond to user queries in a specific technical jargon unique to their industry. They have a dataset of example conversations. Which approach should they consider?',
  'mcq',
  '["Using zero-shot prompting without any examples.","Fine-tuning the model with the industry-specific conversation dataset.","Relying on the model''s default pre-training for general knowledge.","Always choosing the largest, most powerful model for every task."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is the most effective approach. Fine-tuning the foundation model with a dataset of industry-specific conversations will adapt the model to understand and generate responses using the unique technical jargon, significantly improving its performance in that domain. Option A (zero-shot prompting) would likely not be sufficient for niche jargon. Option C (default pre-training) would not provide the specialized understanding needed. Option D (largest model) is an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001) and doesn''t guarantee domain-specific understanding without customization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_3_26',
  'What is the primary goal of ''pre-training'' a foundation model?',
  'mcq',
  '["To adapt the model to a specific downstream task using a small, labeled dataset.","To train the model on a massive, diverse dataset to learn general language patterns and representations.","To optimize the model''s performance for real-time inference latency.","To reduce the number of parameters in the model for efficient deployment."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) describes the primary goal of pre-training: to learn broad language understanding and generation capabilities from vast amounts of unlabeled text data, forming a ''foundation'' for various downstream tasks. Option A describes fine-tuning. Option C and Option D are optimization goals that might be addressed after pre-training or during fine-tuning/deployment.',
