) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_50',
  'What is the primary purpose of post-hoc explainability techniques like SHAP values and feature importance?',
  'mcq',
  '["To simplify the training process of transparent models.","To make black-box models more interpretable after they have been trained.","To automatically retrain models to remove all bias.","To replace the need for human oversight in AI systems."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (To make black-box models more interpretable after they have been trained) is the primary purpose of post-hoc explainability techniques. These techniques are applied *after* a model is built to understand its predictions, especially for complex black-box models. Option A (To simplify the training process of transparent models) is incorrect; these techniques are for interpretation, not training simplification. Option C (To automatically retrain models to remove all bias) is an overstatement; they help *detect* bias but don''t automatically remove it. Option D (To replace the need for human oversight in AI systems) is incorrect; explainability complements, rather than replaces, human oversight.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_51',
  'Regulations like GDPR require organizations to explain automated decisions made by AI systems. What concept does this requirement directly relate to?',
  'mcq',
  '["Model robustness","Data privacy","AI explainability","Algorithmic fairness"]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (AI explainability) is the concept directly related to regulatory requirements like GDPR for explaining automated decisions. It mandates that individuals have the right to understand the reasoning behind decisions affecting them. Option A (Model robustness) is about system resilience. Option B (Data privacy) is about protecting personal data. Option D (Algorithmic fairness) is about equitable outcomes, which is related but explainability is the specific requirement for understanding the ''why''.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_52',
  'A data scientist chooses a deep neural network for a critical application in a highly regulated sector without considering how to explain its decisions. This approach is an anti-pattern. Why is this problematic?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deep neural networks are inherently less accurate.","Black-box models without explainability tools cannot satisfy regulatory requirements.","Transparent models are always easier to train and deploy.","The model will automatically become explainable over time."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (Black-box models without explainability tools cannot satisfy regulatory requirements) correctly identifies why choosing a black-box model for regulated industries without planning for explainability is problematic. Regulations like GDPR often require explanations for automated decisions, which deep learning models cannot provide without specific tooling (e.g., SHAP, SageMaker Clarify). This aligns with anti-pattern `ap-4.2-001`. Option A (Deep neural networks are inherently less accurate) is generally false; they often achieve high accuracy. Option C (Transparent models are always easier to train and deploy) is not necessarily true. Option D (The model will automatically become explainable over time) is incorrect; explainability requires deliberate effort and tools.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_53',
  'Which of the following best describes a ''transparent model'' in AI?',
  'mcq',
  '["A model that is always open source.","A model whose internal workings and decision-making logic are easily understandable.","A model that processes data without any encryption.","A model that achieves perfect accuracy on all tasks."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (A model whose internal workings and decision-making logic are easily understandable) best describes a transparent model. Examples include decision trees or linear regression, where the path to a decision can be directly traced. Option A (A model that is always open source) is not the definition of transparency, though open-source models can be transparent. Option C (A model that processes data without any encryption) is a security anti-pattern, not related to model transparency. Option D (A model that achieves perfect accuracy on all tasks) is an unrealistic ideal and not related to transparency.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_54',
  'What is an example of a black-box model that typically requires post-hoc explainability techniques to understand its decisions?',
  'mcq',
  '["Linear Regression","Decision Tree","Deep Neural Network","Rule-based Expert System"]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (Deep Neural Network) is a classic example of a black-box model due to its complex, non-linear structure, making its internal decision logic difficult to interpret directly. Therefore, post-hoc techniques are often needed. Option A (Linear Regression) and Option B (Decision Tree) are examples of transparent or ''white-box'' models because their decision processes are straightforward to understand. Option D (Rule-based Expert System) is also inherently transparent as its logic is explicitly defined by rules.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_55',
  'To foster user trust in AI systems, especially in sensitive applications, what design principle is recommended for creating explainable AI systems?',
  'mcq',
  '["Machine-centric design","Human-centered design","Algorithm-first design","Data-only design"]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (Human-centered design) is recommended for creating explainable AI systems that users trust. This approach focuses on the needs and understanding of the end-users, ensuring explanations are relevant and accessible to them. Option A (Machine-centric design) and Option C (Algorithm-first design) prioritize technical aspects over user understanding. Option D (Data-only design) focuses solely on data, neglecting the user experience of the AI system.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_56',
  'Why is it important to measure interpretability alongside performance when developing AI models?',
  'mcq',
  '["To ensure that interpretability always takes precedence over performance.","To confirm that the model is always a black-box model.","To balance the need for high accuracy with the ability to understand model decisions.","To eliminate the need for any form of model evaluation."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (To balance the need for high accuracy with the ability to understand model decisions) is why measuring interpretability alongside performance is important. It ensures that while striving for optimal performance, the model''s decisions remain comprehensible, which is crucial for trust and compliance. Option A (To ensure that interpretability always takes precedence over performance) is an extreme view that might lead to underperforming models. Option B (To confirm that the model is always a black-box model) is incorrect; the goal is often to make black-box models more interpretable. Option D (To eliminate the need for any form of model evaluation) is incorrect; interpretability is a form of evaluation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_57',
  'Which of the following is an example of a post-hoc explainability technique used to understand the contribution of individual features to a model''s prediction?',
  'mcq',
  '["Creating a new training dataset","SHAP values","Increasing model complexity","Reducing the number of model parameters"]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (SHAP values) is a well-known post-hoc explainability technique that helps explain the output of any machine learning model by computing the contribution of each feature to the prediction. Option A (Creating a new training dataset) is a data preparation step, not an explainability technique. Option C (Increasing model complexity) generally makes models less explainable. Option D (Reducing the number of model parameters) is a model simplification technique, not a post-hoc explanation method.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_58',
  'A regulatory body requires that AI systems used for public services must be auditable and transparent. Which practice directly supports meeting this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Using only proprietary, closed-source models.","Documenting data provenance and model development with tools like SageMaker Model Cards.","Deploying models without any human oversight.","Maximizing model complexity to improve accuracy."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (Documenting data provenance and model development with tools like SageMaker Model Cards) directly supports auditable and transparent AI systems. Model Cards provide a structured way to document critical information about a model, making it easier to audit and understand. Option A (Using only proprietary, closed-source models) can hinder transparency. Option C (Deploying models without any human oversight) is an anti-pattern for responsible AI, especially in public services. Option D (Maximizing model complexity to improve accuracy) can make models less transparent and harder to audit.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_59',
  'What is the primary benefit of using open-source models and data provenance information for AI systems?',
  'mcq',
  '["To reduce the cost of cloud infrastructure.","To enhance model transparency and foster community trust.","To eliminate the need for model retraining.","To guarantee higher model accuracy than proprietary models."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (To enhance model transparency and foster community trust) is the primary benefit. Open-source models allow for inspection of their code and architecture, while data provenance provides clarity on data origins, both contributing to greater transparency and trust. Option A (To reduce the cost of cloud infrastructure) is not a direct primary benefit. Option C (To eliminate the need for model retraining) is incorrect; models still require retraining. Option D (To guarantee higher model accuracy than proprietary models) is not guaranteed; accuracy depends on many factors.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_60',
  'In the context of AI explainability, what does ''feature importance'' refer to?',
  'mcq',
  '["The total number of features used in a model.","The computational cost of extracting each feature.","The degree to which each input feature contributes to a model''s prediction.","The process of selecting the most relevant features for training."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (The degree to which each input feature contributes to a model''s prediction) accurately defines ''feature importance'' in AI explainability. It quantifies how much each feature influences the model''s output, helping to understand its decision-making. Option A (The total number of features used in a model) is simply a count. Option B (The computational cost of extracting each feature) relates to engineering, not interpretability. Option D (The process of selecting the most relevant features for training) describes feature selection, which is a pre-modeling step.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_61',
  'What is the key difference between a ''transparent model'' and a ''black-box model'' in AI?',
  'mcq',
  '["Transparent models are always more accurate than black-box models.","Transparent models use only structured data, while black-box models use unstructured data.","Transparent models allow direct inspection of their internal logic, while black-box models do not.","Black-box models require more computational resources for training than transparent models."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) highlights the key difference. Transparent models (like decision trees or linear regression) have internal logic that can be easily understood and inspected by humans. Black-box models (like deep neural networks) are complex, making their internal decision-making process opaque and difficult to interpret directly. Option A is incorrect; black-box models often achieve higher accuracy. Option B is incorrect; both types of models can handle various data types. Option D is not universally true; complexity and resource needs vary greatly within both categories.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_62',
  'A company is using a deep neural network for fraud detection in financial transactions. Due to strict financial regulations, they must be able to explain why a specific transaction was flagged as fraudulent. Which AWS service can help provide post-hoc explainability for this black-box model?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Macie","AWS Audit Manager","Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","Amazon SageMaker Clarify"]'::json,
  '{3}'::int[],
  'Option D (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Clarify. It is designed to provide model explainability, including post-hoc techniques like SHAP values and feature importance, for black-box models such as deep neural networks. This helps in understanding why a model made a particular prediction, which is crucial for regulatory compliance in financial services. Option A (Amazon Macie) is for sensitive data discovery. Option B (AWS Audit Manager) is for compliance auditing. Option C (Amazon Bedrock Guardrails) is for content filtering in generative AI. None of these other services provide model explainability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_63',
  'What is the primary purpose of ''post-hoc explainability techniques'' like SHAP values and feature importance?',
  'mcq',
  '["To simplify the architecture of complex deep learning models.","To make black-box models transparent by interpreting their predictions after they have been made.","To improve the training speed of machine learning models.","To automatically generate new features for model training."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes the primary purpose. Post-hoc explainability techniques are applied *after* a model has been trained to interpret its predictions, especially for black-box models where the internal logic is not directly accessible. They help explain ''why'' a model made a specific decision. Option A is incorrect; these techniques don''t simplify the model architecture itself. Option C and D are incorrect; they are not related to training speed or feature generation, but rather to understanding model behavior.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_64',
  'A pharmaceutical company is developing an AI model to predict drug efficacy. Given the highly regulated nature of drug development, they must ensure the model''s predictions are interpretable and can be justified to regulatory bodies. Which anti-pattern should they specifically avoid?',
  'mcq',
  '["Treating responsible AI as an afterthought rather than a design principle.","Deploying AI in high-stakes decisions without human oversight.","Choosing black-box models for regulated industries without explainability requirements planning.","Failing to implement robust data privacy measures for patient data."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) directly addresses the scenario''s core challenge. The anti-pattern [ap-4.2-001] warns against ''Choosing black-box models for regulated industries without explainability requirements planning.'' In highly regulated fields like pharmaceuticals, the ability to explain model decisions is often a regulatory requirement. Deploying an opaque model without a clear strategy for interpretability would be a significant risk. Option A and B are general responsible AI anti-patterns but less specific to the explainability requirement in regulated industries. Option D is a privacy concern, not directly about model explainability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_65',
  'Which of the following is a characteristic of a ''black-box'' AI model?',
  'mcq',
  '["Its internal workings are easily understood and can be directly inspected.","It is typically a simple linear regression or decision tree model.","Its decision-making process is complex and difficult for humans to interpret directly.","It can only be used for classification tasks, not regression."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) describes a key characteristic of a black-box AI model. These models, often deep neural networks, have intricate internal structures that make their decision-making process opaque and challenging for humans to understand directly. Option A describes a transparent model. Option B lists examples of transparent models. Option D is incorrect; black-box models can be used for both classification and regression tasks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_66',
  'A data scientist is evaluating several machine learning models for a critical application. They need to choose a model that inherently allows for direct inspection of its decision-making logic without requiring additional tools. Which type of model would best fit this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["A complex deep neural network.","A gradient boosting machine.","A simple decision tree.","A large language model."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) is a simple decision tree. Decision trees are considered transparent models because their internal logic (the sequence of rules applied to reach a decision) can be directly visualized and easily understood by humans. Options A, B, and D (deep neural networks, gradient boosting machines, and large language models) are typically black-box models whose internal workings are complex and not directly interpretable without post-hoc techniques.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_67',
  'Which AWS service can be used to generate ''model cards'' that document model details, performance metrics, and responsible AI considerations, thereby enhancing transparency?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Macie","Amazon SageMaker Clarify","AWS Audit Manager","Amazon SageMaker Model Cards"]'::json,
  '{3}'::int[],
  'Option D (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Model Cards. Model Cards are a feature within Amazon SageMaker that allow users to create standardized documentation for their ML models, detailing their purpose, performance, training data, ethical considerations, and more, thus directly enhancing transparency and governance. Option A (Amazon Macie) is for sensitive data discovery. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Clarify) is for bias detection and explainability. Option C (AWS Audit Manager) is for compliance auditing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_68',
  'What does the term ''model interpretability'' primarily refer to in the context of AI?',
  'mcq',
  '["The speed at which a model can make predictions.","The ability to understand how a model arrives at its predictions.","The ease with which a model can be deployed to production.","The capacity of a model to generalize to unseen data."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) defines model interpretability. It refers to the extent to which humans can understand the reasoning behind a model''s decisions or predictions. This is a core aspect of explainable AI (XAI). Option A describes inference speed. Option C describes deployment ease. Option D describes generalization, which is related to model performance, not interpretability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_69',
  'When designing an AI system, what is a key benefit of applying human-centered design principles to create explainable AI systems?',
  'mcq',
  '["It allows for the exclusive use of black-box models without any explainability tools.","It ensures that the AI system''s performance metrics are always maximized.","It helps build user trust and ensures explanations are relevant and understandable to the target audience.","It eliminates the need for any human oversight in high-stakes decision-making."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) is a key benefit of human-centered design for explainable AI. By focusing on the user''s needs and understanding, explanations can be tailored to be relevant, comprehensible, and ultimately build trust in the AI system. Option A is incorrect; human-centered design for explainability aims to make models understandable, not to justify using black-box models without tools. Option B is incorrect; while performance is important, human-centered design prioritizes usability and understanding. Option D is incorrect and contradicts the need for human oversight in high-stakes scenarios.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_70',
  'A company is developing an AI system for automated loan approvals. To comply with consumer protection laws that require transparency in credit decisions, they need to implement techniques that reveal the influence of individual features on the model''s output. Which post-hoc explainability technique is suitable for this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Data augmentation","Hyperparameter tuning","SHAP values","Cross-validation"]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) refers to SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) values, which are a post-hoc explainability technique used to quantify the contribution of each feature to a model''s individual prediction. This is highly suitable for explaining why a specific loan application was approved or denied, meeting transparency requirements. Option A (data augmentation) is a technique to increase data diversity. Option B (hyperparameter tuning) is for optimizing model performance. Option D (cross-validation) is for evaluating model generalization. None of these other options are explainability techniques.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_71',
  'What is a primary challenge when balancing model safety with transparency in AI systems?',
  'mcq',
  '["Transparent models are inherently less safe than black-box models.","Making a model more transparent can sometimes reveal vulnerabilities that could be exploited.","Safety measures always reduce model accuracy, making transparency irrelevant.","Transparency is only relevant for models deployed in non-critical applications."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) identifies a primary challenge. While transparency is crucial for understanding and trust, revealing too much about a model''s internal workings or decision boundaries could potentially expose it to adversarial attacks or manipulation, thus impacting its safety or robustness. Balancing these two aspects requires careful consideration. Option A is incorrect; safety is not inherently tied to transparency in that way. Option C is incorrect; safety measures don''t always reduce accuracy, and transparency remains relevant. Option D is incorrect; transparency is often more critical in critical applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_72',
  'Which statement accurately describes the role of ''data provenance'' in achieving transparent and explainable AI models?',
  'mcq',
  '["It refers to the computational resources used to train the model.","It documents the origin, transformations, and characteristics of the data used to train the model.","It is a technique for compressing model size to improve inference speed.","It describes the legal framework governing the deployment of AI systems."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes data provenance. It involves documenting the entire lifecycle of the data, including its origin, how it was collected, processed, and transformed. This information is crucial for understanding potential biases, ensuring data quality, and contributing to the overall transparency and explainability of an AI model. Option A, C, and D are unrelated to data provenance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_73',
  'An organization is considering using a pre-trained large language model (LLM) for customer support. The LLM is a black-box model. To meet internal governance requirements for understanding model behavior, what should the organization prioritize alongside model performance?',
  'mcq',
  '["Minimizing the number of features used by the LLM.","Implementing post-hoc explainability techniques to interpret LLM outputs.","Reducing the LLM''s computational footprint for cost savings.","Ensuring the LLM is trained on the largest possible dataset."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate action. Since LLMs are black-box models, implementing post-hoc explainability techniques (like those offered by Amazon SageMaker Clarify or open-source tools) is crucial to interpret their outputs and understand why certain responses are generated, thus meeting governance requirements for understanding model behavior. This directly addresses the challenge of using black-box models responsibly. Option A and C are related to model efficiency and complexity but not directly to interpretability. Option D is about data volume for training, which doesn''t inherently make a black-box model explainable.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_74',
  'Which of the following is an example of a ''transparent model'' in AI?',
  'mcq',
  '["A complex convolutional neural network for image recognition.","A linear regression model used for predicting house prices.","A generative adversarial network (GAN) for creating synthetic images.","A transformer-based large language model for text generation."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is a linear regression model. Linear regression models are considered transparent because their decision-making process is straightforward and directly interpretable: the output is a linear combination of the input features, with clear coefficients indicating feature importance. Options A, C, and D (convolutional neural networks, GANs, and transformer-based LLMs) are all examples of complex deep learning models that are typically considered black-box models due to their intricate and opaque internal workings.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_75',
  'Which AWS service provides capabilities for post-hoc explainability techniques, such as SHAP values and feature importance, to help data scientists and developers understand the predictions of complex machine learning models?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Audit Manager","Amazon Macie","Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","Amazon SageMaker Clarify"]'::json,
  '{3}'::int[],
  'Option D (Amazon SageMaker Clarify) is the correct service for providing post-hoc explainability techniques like SHAP values and feature importance for machine learning models. It helps users understand why a model made a particular prediction. Option A (AWS Audit Manager) is used for managing and automating compliance audits. Option B (Amazon Macie) is a data security and data privacy service that uses machine learning to discover, classify, and protect sensitive data. Option C (Amazon Bedrock Guardrails) is designed to implement safeguards and content policies for generative AI applications, not for explaining the predictions of traditional ML models.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_76',
  'What is a defining characteristic of a ''black-box'' AI model, such as a deep neural network, when compared to a transparent model like a decision tree?',
  'mcq',
  '["Its internal decision-making process is difficult to understand.","It always achieves higher accuracy than transparent models.","It requires less training data to achieve optimal performance.","It is inherently compliant with all regulatory explainability requirements."]'::json,
  '{0}'::int[],
  'Option A (Its internal decision-making process is difficult to understand) is the defining characteristic of a ''black-box'' AI model. These models, especially deep neural networks, have complex internal structures that make it challenging for humans to interpret how they arrive at a particular prediction. Option B (It always achieves higher accuracy than transparent models) is not universally true; while often powerful, their accuracy depends on the problem and data. Option C (It requires less training data to achieve optimal performance) is incorrect; deep learning models typically require large amounts of data. Option D (It is inherently compliant with all regulatory explainability requirements) is incorrect and represents an anti-pattern (ap-4.2-001); black-box models often struggle with regulatory explainability requirements without additional tooling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_8_m_8_2_77',
  'A healthcare provider plans to use a complex deep learning model for patient diagnosis recommendations. Due to strict regulatory requirements (e.g., GDPR), they must be able to explain the reasoning behind the model''s predictions. Which approach best addresses this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploying the deep learning model without specific explainability tools, assuming high accuracy is sufficient.","Integrating Amazon SageMaker Clarify into the model development and deployment pipeline.","Using Amazon Macie to identify PII in the training data, which ensures explainability.","Relying solely on Amazon Bedrock Guardrails to filter model outputs for safety."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (Integrating Amazon SageMaker Clarify into the model development and deployment pipeline) is the best approach. Amazon SageMaker Clarify provides tools for model explainability, allowing the healthcare provider to understand and articulate the factors influencing the deep learning model''s diagnosis recommendations, which is crucial for regulatory compliance. Option A (Deploying the deep learning model without specific explainability tools, assuming high accuracy is sufficient) is an anti-pattern (ap-4.2-001). High accuracy does not equate to explainability, and regulatory bodies often demand transparency for automated decisions, especially in high-stakes domains like healthcare. Option C (Using Amazon Macie to identify PII in the training data, which ensures explainability) is incorrect; Amazon Macie is for data privacy and sensitive data discovery, not model explainability. Option D (Relying solely on Amazon Bedrock Guardrails to filter model outputs for safety) is also incorrect; Amazon Bedrock Guardrails is for content moderation and safety policies in generative AI applications, not for explaining the internal logic of a deep learning diagnostic model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain8_1',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_8_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

