INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_82',
  'A data scientist is preparing to develop a new machine learning model. Order the fundamental stages of the ML development lifecycle from beginning to end.',
  'ordering',
  '["Model training","Model evaluation","Model deployment","Data collection"]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}'::int[],
  'The machine learning development lifecycle begins with data. Option D (Data collection) is the initial step to gather relevant data. This is followed by Option A (Model training), where the model learns from the prepared data. After training, Option B (Model evaluation) assesses the model''s performance. Finally, Option C (Model deployment) makes the trained model available for inference.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_83',
  'A machine learning engineer is outlining the high-level stages of a typical ML development lifecycle for a new project. Order these fundamental stages chronologically.',
  'ordering',
  '["Model Training and Evaluation","Data Collection and Preparation","Model Deployment and Monitoring","Feature Engineering"]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}'::int[],
  'Data Collection and Preparation (Option B) is the initial phase, involving gathering raw data and making it ready for use. Feature Engineering (Option D) transforms raw data into features that are suitable for machine learning models. Model Training and Evaluation (Option A) is where the model learns from the prepared data and its performance is assessed. Finally, Model Deployment and Monitoring (Option C) makes the model available for predictions in a production environment and continuously tracks its performance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_84',
  'After training an ML model, a data analyst needs to evaluate its performance on unseen data. Order the typical progression of evaluating a classification model''s performance from initial assessment to deeper understanding.',
  'ordering',
  '["Calculate Precision and Recall for specific classes","Split data into training, validation, and test sets","Compute overall Accuracy on the test set","Analyze the Confusion Matrix"]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}'::int[],
  'Split data into training, validation, and test sets (Option B) is a foundational step to ensure unbiased evaluation. Compute overall Accuracy on the test set (Option C) provides a quick, high-level understanding of the model''s correctness. Analyze the Confusion Matrix (Option D) offers a more detailed view of correct and incorrect predictions for each class. Finally, Calculate Precision and Recall for specific classes (Option A) provides crucial insights into the model''s performance on individual classes, especially important in imbalanced datasets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_85',
  'A data science team has successfully trained an ML model using Amazon SageMaker and now needs to deploy it for real-time predictions. Order the steps to deploy a model as an Amazon SageMaker endpoint for inference.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a SageMaker Model resource","Invoke the endpoint for predictions","Create a SageMaker Endpoint Configuration","Create a SageMaker Endpoint"]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}'::int[],
  'Create a SageMaker Model resource (Option A) registers the trained model artifacts and Docker image for inference. Create a SageMaker Endpoint Configuration (Option C) specifies the compute resources (instance type, count) and model(s) to be deployed. Create a SageMaker Endpoint (Option D) provisions the actual infrastructure based on the configuration, making the model accessible via an API. Invoke the endpoint for predictions (Option B) is the final step where applications send data to the endpoint to receive real-time inferences.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_86',
  'After deploying an ML model into production, a MLOps engineer observes that its performance is starting to degrade over time due to changes in incoming data. Order the steps to address this model drift and maintain performance.',
  'ordering',
  '["Redeploy the updated model","Monitor model performance metrics","Detect significant model drift","Retrain the model with fresh data"]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}'::int[],
  'Monitor model performance metrics (Option B) is the continuous process of tracking how the model performs in production. Detect significant model drift (Option C) involves identifying when the model''s performance has degraded beyond an acceptable threshold. Retrain the model with fresh data (Option D) is the corrective action, using new, representative data to update the model''s learning. Finally, Redeploy the updated model (Option A) replaces the old, underperforming model with the newly trained one to restore performance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_87',
  'A data science team is developing a new fraud detection model using Amazon SageMaker. They have collected raw transaction data in Amazon S3 and need to prepare it for training, then deploy the model for real-time inference. The goal is to ensure data quality and model effectiveness. Order the logical steps they should follow from data preparation to initial deployment.',
  'ordering',
  '["Perform feature engineering to create new predictive variables","Deploy the trained model to a SageMaker endpoint for inference","Preprocess and clean the raw data (e.g., handle missing values, outliers)","Train the machine learning model using the prepared dataset"]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}'::int[],
  'The ML development lifecycle begins with data preparation. Option C (Preprocess and clean the raw data) is the initial step to ensure data quality. Following this, Option A (Perform feature engineering) is crucial to transform raw data into features that improve model performance. After data is prepared, Option D (Train the machine learning model) is performed. Finally, Option B (Deploy the trained model) makes the model available for real-time predictions. Skipping data preprocessing or feature engineering (anti-pattern ap-1.3-002) would lead to poor model quality.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_88',
  'A company is building a personalized product recommendation system for its e-commerce platform using Amazon Personalize. After initial data ingestion into Personalize, they need to configure the system to generate recommendations for users. Order the steps for setting up and using Amazon Personalize to deliver recommendations.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a solution and a solution version (training a model)","Import interaction data into Amazon Personalize datasets","Create a campaign to deploy the solution version","Get personalized recommendations using the campaign"]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'To use Amazon Personalize, the first step is to ingest the relevant data. Option B (Import interaction data) is necessary to provide Personalize with user-item interactions. Once data is available, Option A (Create a solution and a solution version) involves defining the recommendation recipe and training the underlying model. After a model is trained, Option C (Create a campaign) deploys the solution version, making it available for real-time inference. Finally, Option D (Get personalized recommendations) allows the application to query the campaign for recommendations. Skipping data ingestion or campaign creation would prevent the system from generating recommendations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_89',
  'An ML engineer is tasked with iteratively improving an existing Amazon SageMaker model that is showing signs of concept drift in production. They need to collect new data, retrain the model, and then evaluate its performance before updating the production endpoint to ensure continuous accuracy. Order the steps for this iterative model improvement process.',
  'ordering',
  '["Evaluate the retrained model''s performance on a new test set","Collect and label new data reflecting current patterns","Update the production SageMaker endpoint with the new model version","Retrain the machine learning model using the updated dataset"]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}'::int[],
  'When a model shows concept drift, the process starts with updating the data. Option B (Collect and label new data) is the first step to capture the shifted data distribution. Next, Option D (Retrain the machine learning model) uses this new data to create an updated model. Following retraining, Option A (Evaluate the retrained model''s performance) is critical to ensure the new model is an improvement and generalizes well. Only after successful evaluation should Option C (Update the production SageMaker endpoint) be performed to replace the old model. Treating model deployment as the end of the ML lifecycle (anti-pattern ap-1.3-003) would lead to degraded performance over time.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_90',
  'A team is preparing a large, raw dataset stored in Amazon S3 for training a classification model to predict customer churn. The raw data contains various data types, including missing values and categorical attributes. They need to clean, transform, and select relevant attributes before feeding it into a SageMaker training job. Order the typical data preparation steps.',
  'ordering',
  '["Handle missing values and outliers in the dataset","Select relevant features and reduce dimensionality","Encode categorical variables into numerical representations","Perform feature scaling (e.g., normalization or standardization)"]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}'::int[],
  'Effective data preparation is fundamental for model performance. Option A (Handle missing values and outliers) is typically the first step to ensure data integrity and prevent errors. Following this, Option C (Encode categorical variables) converts non-numerical data into a format suitable for most ML algorithms. Then, Option D (Perform feature scaling) standardizes the range of independent features, which is important for many algorithms. Finally, Option B (Select relevant features) helps reduce noise and improve model efficiency. Skipping data preprocessing and feature engineering (anti-pattern ap-1.3-002) would severely degrade model quality.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_91',
  'A data scientist has trained an Amazon SageMaker model for detecting rare events, such as system anomalies, and needs to rigorously evaluate its performance before deployment. Given the inherent class imbalance in anomaly detection datasets, relying solely on accuracy can be misleading. Order the model evaluation steps they should prioritize to get a comprehensive understanding of the model''s effectiveness.',
  'ordering',
  '["Calculate overall accuracy on the test set","Compute precision, recall, and F1-score for the minority class","Analyze the ROC curve and calculate the Area Under the Curve (AUC)","Generate a confusion matrix to visualize true positives, negatives, false positives, and false negatives"]'::json,
  '{3,1,2,0}'::int[],
  'When dealing with imbalanced datasets, a comprehensive evaluation is crucial. Option D (Generate a confusion matrix) provides a foundational visual breakdown of model predictions, which is essential for understanding performance across classes. Building on this, Option B (Compute precision, recall, and F1-score for the minority class) directly addresses the performance on the rare events, which accuracy alone would miss. Option C (Analyze the ROC curve and calculate AUC) provides a robust measure of the model''s ability to distinguish between classes across various thresholds. Finally, Option A (Calculate overall accuracy) can be considered, but it should be interpreted with caution due to class imbalance. Optimizing for accuracy alone regardless of class imbalance (anti-pattern ap-1.3-001) is a common pitfall that these steps help avoid.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_92',
  'A machine learning engineer is developing a new predictive model. Order the standard stages of the ML development pipeline from initial data handling to making the model ready for use.',
  'ordering',
  '["Model training with prepared features","Data collection and initial exploration","Feature engineering and preprocessing","Model evaluation and validation"]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}'::int[],
  'The ML pipeline begins with Option B (Data collection and initial exploration) to gather and understand the raw data. This is followed by Option C (Feature engineering and preprocessing), which cleans, transforms, and selects relevant features from the raw data. Only after data preparation is complete can Option A (Model training with prepared features) occur. Finally, Option D (Model evaluation and validation) assesses the model''s performance before deployment. Skipping data preprocessing and feature engineering (Option C) is a common anti-pattern that severely degrades model quality.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_93',
  'After training a classification model, a data scientist needs to thoroughly evaluate its performance, especially for a dataset with imbalanced classes. Order the evaluation steps to gain a comprehensive understanding of the model''s effectiveness.',
  'ordering',
  '["Calculate overall accuracy","Analyze the confusion matrix","Review precision, recall, and F1-score for each class","Compare performance against a simple baseline model"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'While Option A (Calculate overall accuracy) is a common starting point, it can be misleading with imbalanced datasets. Therefore, the next crucial step is Option B (Analyze the confusion matrix), which provides a detailed breakdown of true positives, false positives, true negatives, and false negatives. This leads to Option C (Review precision, recall, and F1-score for each class), which are more robust metrics for imbalanced datasets. Finally, Option D (Compare performance against a simple baseline model) provides context on whether the complex ML model offers significant improvement. Optimizing for accuracy alone without considering other metrics or class imbalance is a common anti-pattern.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_94',
  'Machine learning model development is an iterative process. After an initial model is trained and evaluated, further improvements are often necessary. Order the typical steps in this iterative refinement cycle.',
  'ordering',
  '["Evaluate the model''s performance on validation data","Adjust model hyperparameters or features","Train an initial version of the model","Retrain the model with updated configurations"]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}'::int[],
  'The iterative cycle starts with Option C (Train an initial version of the model). Following this, Option A (Evaluate the model''s performance on validation data) assesses its effectiveness. Based on the evaluation results, Option B (Adjust model hyperparameters or features) is performed to address any shortcomings. Finally, Option D (Retrain the model with updated configurations) applies these adjustments to create an improved model, restarting the cycle. Deploying a model after the first training without iteration is an anti-pattern that often leads to suboptimal performance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'medium'
