) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_71',
  'A company is developing a new product recommendation system using a supervised machine learning approach. Order the following steps in the typical process of building and using a supervised learning model.',
  'ordering',
  '["Model Training with labeled data","Data Collection and Labeling","Model Deployment for predictions","Feature Engineering and Preprocessing"]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}'::int[],
  'Data Collection and Labeling (Option B) is the initial step where raw data is gathered and annotated with the correct output, which is crucial for supervised learning. Feature Engineering and Preprocessing (Option D) involves transforming raw data into a suitable format for the model and creating relevant features. Model Training with labeled data (Option A) is where the algorithm learns patterns from the prepared, labeled dataset. Finally, Model Deployment for predictions (Option C) makes the trained model available to make inferences on new, unseen data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_72',
  'A development team plans to leverage a Foundation Model (FM) to create a custom text summarization tool. Order the typical high-level stages involved in adapting and using an FM for a specific downstream task.',
  'ordering',
  '["Pre-training on vast datasets","Fine-tuning with task-specific data","Inference for new summarization requests","Prompt Engineering for desired output"]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}'::int[],
  'Pre-training on vast datasets (Option A) is the initial, resource-intensive stage where the foundation model learns general patterns and knowledge. Fine-tuning with task-specific data (Option B) adapts the pre-trained model to a particular downstream task, like summarization, using a smaller, labeled dataset. Prompt Engineering for desired output (Option D) involves crafting effective inputs to guide the fine-tuned model to generate the desired summarization. Finally, Inference for new summarization requests (Option C) is when the adapted model processes new input text to produce summaries.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_73',
  'A retail company wants to automatically classify product images uploaded by users into predefined categories using a pre-trained computer vision model. Order the conceptual steps involved in this image classification process.',
  'ordering',
  '["Extract features from the image","Input the image data","Output the predicted category label","Apply a classification algorithm"]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}'::int[],
  'Input the image data (Option B) is the first step where the raw image pixels are fed into the model. Extract features from the image (Option A) involves the model identifying relevant patterns and characteristics (e.g., edges, textures) from the input. Apply a classification algorithm (Option D) uses these extracted features to determine which predefined category the image belongs to. Finally, Output the predicted category label (Option C) provides the classification result to the user or system.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_74',
  'A new team member is learning about the hierarchy of AI concepts and is trying to understand how Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning, and Deep Learning relate to each other. They need to grasp the scope and relationship of these fields. Order these concepts from the broadest and most encompassing field to the most specific sub-field.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deep Learning","Artificial Intelligence","Machine Learning"]'::json,
  '{1,2,0}'::int[],
  'The relationship between these terms is hierarchical. Option B (Artificial Intelligence) is the broadest concept, encompassing any technique that enables computers to mimic human intelligence. Option C (Machine Learning) is a subset of AI that focuses on systems learning from data without explicit programming. Option A (Deep Learning) is a specialized subset of Machine Learning that uses neural networks with many layers. Treating AI and machine learning as interchangeable terms (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001) is a common misconception this ordering clarifies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_75',
  'A data scientist is explaining the process of training a supervised machine learning model to a business stakeholder. The stakeholder needs to understand the logical flow from initial data input to the final model output. Order the key concepts in a typical supervised learning training process.',
  'ordering',
  '["Model training with labeled data","Feature extraction from raw data","Dataset collection with labels","Model evaluation using a validation set"]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}'::int[],
  'In supervised learning, the process begins with data. Option C (Dataset collection with labels) is the fundamental first step, as supervised learning requires labeled examples. Next, Option B (Feature extraction) transforms this raw data into a format suitable for the model. Then, Option A (Model training with labeled data) uses these features and labels to learn patterns. Finally, Option D (Model evaluation) assesses the model''s performance on unseen data. Confusing supervised and unsupervised learning (anti-pattern ap-1.1-002) would lead to incorrect assumptions about the necessity of labeled data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_76',
  'A company is exploring different types of machine learning paradigms for various business problems, ranging from predicting customer churn to segmenting customer groups. They need to understand the fundamental differences in how models learn from data based on the availability of labels and feedback. Order the machine learning types from those that primarily require labeled data to those that typically work with unlabeled data.',
  'ordering',
  '["Reinforcement Learning","Supervised Learning","Unsupervised Learning"]'::json,
  '{1,0,2}'::int[],
  'The distinction between ML types often hinges on data labeling. Option B (Supervised Learning) explicitly requires labeled training data to learn a mapping from inputs to outputs. Option A (Reinforcement Learning) learns through trial and error by receiving rewards or penalties, which are a form of feedback but not explicit labels in the same way as supervised learning. Option C (Unsupervised Learning) is designed to find patterns and structures in unlabeled data. Confusing supervised and unsupervised learning (anti-pattern ap-1.1-002) is a common error that this ordering clarifies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_77',
  'An architect is evaluating the capabilities of modern AI systems, particularly those that can generate new content versus those that classify existing data. They are comparing discriminative AI with generative AI for various tasks. Order the following AI capabilities from those that primarily classify or predict based on input data to those that primarily create new content.',
  'ordering',
  '["Image generation from text descriptions","Sentiment analysis of customer reviews","Object detection in surveillance footage","Text summarization of long documents"]'::json,
  '{2,1,3,0}'::int[],
  'AI capabilities can be broadly categorized. Option C (Object detection) is a discriminative task, identifying and localizing objects within an image. Option B (Sentiment analysis) is also typically discriminative, classifying text into positive, negative, or neutral sentiment. Option D (Text summarization) can be discriminative (extracting key sentences) or generative (creating new summary text), but modern approaches often involve generating new content. Option A (Image generation) is a purely generative task, creating novel images from input prompts. Assuming generative AI and discriminative AI are the same (anti-pattern ap-1.1-003) is a misconception this ordering helps to address.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_78',
  'A data scientist is preparing to build a machine learning model. Order the fundamental conceptual steps required to move from raw data to making predictions with a deployed model.',
  'ordering',
  '["Gather and prepare a dataset","Deploy the trained model for inference","Train a machine learning model","Evaluate model performance"]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}'::int[],
  'The correct sequence for a fundamental machine learning workflow starts with Option A (Gather and prepare a dataset), as data is the foundation for any ML project. Next, Option C (Train a machine learning model) uses this prepared data to learn patterns. After training, Option D (Evaluate model performance) is crucial to assess how well the model generalizes. Finally, Option B (Deploy the trained model for inference) makes the model available to make predictions on new, unseen data. Incorrect orders often confuse the sequence of training, evaluation, and deployment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_79',
  'A company wants to develop a machine learning solution to predict customer churn. They have historical customer data that includes whether each customer churned or not. Order the typical steps for building a supervised learning model for this task.',
  'ordering',
  '["Train the model using the labeled dataset","Collect historical customer data and label churn instances","Make predictions on new customer data","Evaluate the model''s accuracy and performance metrics"]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}'::int[],
  'For a supervised learning task like customer churn prediction, the first step is Option B (Collect historical customer data and label churn instances), as supervised learning requires labeled data. Once the data is ready, Option A (Train the model using the labeled dataset) is performed to learn patterns. After training, Option D (Evaluate the model''s accuracy and performance metrics) is essential to understand how well the model performs. Only after satisfactory evaluation can the model proceed to Option C (Make predictions on new customer data) in a production environment. Skipping labeling or evaluating before training would lead to an ineffective model, reflecting a common misconception about supervised learning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_80',
  'Artificial intelligence encompasses various fields and techniques. Order the following concepts from the broadest and most encompassing to the most specific subset.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deep Learning","Machine Learning","Artificial Intelligence"]'::json,
  '{2,1,0}'::int[],
  'The correct hierarchy begins with Option C (Artificial Intelligence) as the broadest concept, representing machines simulating human intelligence. Option B (Machine Learning) is a subset of AI that focuses on systems learning from data without explicit programming. Option A (Deep Learning) is a further specialized subset of Machine Learning that uses neural networks with multiple layers to learn complex patterns. Confusing this hierarchy, such as treating AI and Machine Learning as interchangeable, is a common misconception.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_81',
  'A retail company wants to categorize customer feedback into positive, negative, or neutral sentiments. Order the general steps involved in setting up a classification task for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Gather and preprocess customer feedback text","Define the sentiment categories (positive, negative, neutral)","Train a classification model on labeled feedback","Use the model to predict sentiment for new feedback"]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'For a classification task, the first logical step is Option B (Define the sentiment categories), as this establishes what the model will predict. Then, Option A (Gather and preprocess customer feedback text) is necessary to prepare the data according to these categories. Following this, Option C (Train a classification model on labeled feedback) uses the prepared data to learn the classification patterns. Finally, Option D (Use the model to predict sentiment for new feedback) applies the trained model to new, unseen data. Training a model before defining the target classes would be an illogical approach.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_82',
  'A marketing team wants to generate personalized product descriptions based on customer preferences using a Large Language Model (LLM). Order the steps to leverage an existing Foundation Model for this generative AI task.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy the fine-tuned model for text generation","Select a pre-trained Foundation Model (e.g., from Amazon SageMaker JumpStart)","Fine-tune the model with examples of desired product descriptions","Generate personalized product descriptions"]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}'::int[],
  'The process begins with Option B (Select a pre-trained Foundation Model), as LLMs are often built upon these powerful base models. Next, Option C (Fine-tune the model with examples of desired product descriptions) adapts the general model to the specific task and style. After fine-tuning, Option A (Deploy the fine-tuned model for text generation) makes the model accessible for use. Only then can Option D (Generate personalized product descriptions) be performed. Attempting to generate content before fine-tuning and deployment would not yield task-specific results, highlighting the difference between a general FM and a task-specific generative model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

