INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_73',
  'An e-commerce company wants to enhance its product recommendation engine using generative AI. They are evaluating different strategies to incorporate their vast product catalog and customer review data into the LLM''s knowledge base. They need to select the most appropriate strategy (RAG, fine-tuning, or prompt engineering) for incorporating proprietary data into an LLM for product recommendations, considering cost, effort, and data freshness. Order the following steps to evaluate and choose an LLM customization strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Implement Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) by creating a knowledge base from product data and using it to ground LLM responses.","Start with advanced prompt engineering techniques (e.g., few-shot, chain-of-thought) to guide a base LLM.","Evaluate the performance and cost-effectiveness of the chosen strategy against defined metrics.","Consider fine-tuning a foundation model if prompt engineering and RAG do not meet performance requirements, acknowledging higher cost and effort."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}'::int[],
  'The correct sequence for evaluating and choosing an LLM customization strategy typically starts with the least expensive and most flexible options. Option B (Start with advanced prompt engineering techniques (e.g., few-shot, chain-of-thought) to guide a base LLM) is the initial, low-cost approach. Option A (Implement Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) by creating a knowledge base from product data and using it to ground LLM responses) is the next logical step for incorporating proprietary data without retraining. Option D (Consider fine-tuning a foundation model if prompt engineering and RAG do not meet performance requirements, acknowledging higher cost and effort) is a more resource-intensive option to be considered if simpler methods fail. Finally, Option C (Evaluate the performance and cost-effectiveness of the chosen strategy against defined metrics) is crucial for validating the selected approach. This process helps avoid the anti-pattern [ap-2.2-001] by prioritizing simpler solutions before fine-tuning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_74',
  'A manufacturing company wants to deploy an internal chatbot to assist engineers with troubleshooting and accessing equipment manuals. They initially considered using a base LLM but realized it lacked company-specific knowledge, leading to generic and unhelpful responses. The constraint is to ground an enterprise chatbot with company-specific knowledge to provide accurate and relevant responses. Order the following steps to implement a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) solution for this enterprise chatbot.',
  'ordering',
  '["Integrate the RAG system with the chatbot interface, allowing user queries to trigger retrieval and LLM generation.","Extract and chunk proprietary equipment manuals and troubleshooting guides into manageable text segments.","Create vector embeddings for the text chunks and store them in a vector database (e.g., Amazon OpenSearch Service).","Configure an LLM to receive user queries, retrieve relevant document chunks from the vector database, and generate a grounded response."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}'::int[],
  'The correct sequence for implementing a RAG solution for an enterprise chatbot begins with preparing the proprietary data. Option B (Extract and chunk proprietary equipment manuals and troubleshooting guides into manageable text segments) is the initial data processing step. Option C (Create vector embeddings for the text chunks and store them in a vector database (e.g., Amazon OpenSearch Service)) follows, as vector embeddings are essential for semantic search. Option D (Configure an LLM to receive user queries, retrieve relevant document chunks from the vector database, and generate a grounded response) sets up the core RAG logic. Finally, Option A (Integrate the RAG system with the chatbot interface, allowing user queries to trigger retrieval and LLM generation) makes the solution accessible to users. This approach directly addresses the anti-pattern [ap-2.2-002] by grounding the base model with company-specific knowledge.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_75',
  'A logistics company wants to automate its customer service inquiries, which often involve checking order status, updating delivery addresses, and initiating returns. These tasks require interacting with multiple internal systems and APIs. The constraint is to automate complex, multi-system customer service tasks using generative AI. Order the following steps to design and implement an agentic AI workflow with tool use.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the specific tools (APIs or Lambda functions) that the agent can invoke for tasks like ''checkOrderStatus'' or ''updateDeliveryAddress''.","Orchestrate the agent''s behavior, defining its overall goal, step-by-step instructions, and how it should use the available tools.","Integrate the agent into the customer service platform, allowing it to receive and respond to inquiries.","Identify the complex, multi-step customer service tasks that require interaction with external systems."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}'::int[],
  'The correct sequence for designing and implementing an agentic AI workflow with tool use starts with identifying the problem. Option D (Identify the complex, multi-step customer service tasks that require interaction with external systems) is the initial analysis. Option A (Define the specific tools (APIs or Lambda functions) that the agent can invoke for tasks like ''checkOrderStatus'' or ''updateDeliveryAddress'') is next, as the agent needs capabilities. Option B (Orchestrate the agent''s behavior, defining its overall goal, step-by-step instructions, and how it should use the available tools) sets up the agent''s reasoning and execution logic. Finally, Option C (Integrate the agent into the customer service platform, allowing it to receive and respond to inquiries) deploys the agent for practical use.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_76',
  'A news organization is using an LLM for generating news summaries and drafting articles. They are concerned about the model''s knowledge cutoff, potential for hallucinations, and context window limitations, which could impact journalistic integrity and accuracy. The constraint is to mitigate common limitations of generative AI when deploying for sensitive business applications. Order the following steps to address key limitations of LLMs in a production environment.',
  'ordering',
  '["Implement a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) system to provide the LLM with up-to-date and verifiable information.","Establish a human-in-the-loop review process for all high-stakes content generated by the LLM.","Employ chunking strategies and context window management techniques for processing long articles or documents.","Regularly audit LLM outputs for factual accuracy, bias, and adherence to journalistic standards."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}'::int[],
  'The correct sequence for addressing key limitations of LLMs in a production environment involves a multi-faceted approach. Option A (Implement a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) system to provide the LLM with up-to-date and verifiable information) directly addresses knowledge cutoff and reduces hallucinations. Option C (Employ chunking strategies and context window management techniques for processing long articles or documents) tackles context window limitations. Option B (Establish a human-in-the-loop review process for all high-stakes content generated by the LLM) is a critical safeguard against hallucinations and inaccuracies, especially in sensitive domains. Finally, Option D (Regularly audit LLM outputs for factual accuracy, bias, and adherence to journalistic standards) ensures ongoing quality control and continuous improvement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_77',
  'A marketing department has implemented a generative AI tool for campaign content creation. They need to demonstrate the return on investment (ROI) and overall business impact of this solution to stakeholders. The constraint is to quantify the business value and effectiveness of a generative AI application. Order the following steps to measure the business value and impact of a generative AI solution.',
  'ordering',
  '["Collect data on key performance indicators (KPIs) such as content creation time, engagement rates, and conversion rates.","Define clear business objectives and measurable KPIs for the generative AI application (e.g., increased efficiency, higher customer engagement).","Analyze the collected data to calculate ROI and identify areas for further optimization.","Establish a baseline by measuring KPIs before implementing the generative AI solution."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}'::int[],
  'The correct sequence for measuring the business value and impact of a generative AI solution starts with clear objectives. Option B (Define clear business objectives and measurable KPIs for the generative AI application (e.g., increased efficiency, higher customer engagement)) sets the framework. Option D (Establish a baseline by measuring KPIs before implementing the generative AI solution) provides a crucial point of comparison. Option A (Collect data on key performance indicators (KPIs) such as content creation time, engagement rates, and conversion rates) gathers the necessary information after implementation. Finally, Option C (Analyze the collected data to calculate ROI and identify areas for further optimization) interprets the results and informs future decisions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_78',
  'A software development company is choosing a foundation model from Amazon Bedrock for a new code generation feature in their IDE. They need to consider factors like model capabilities, performance, cost, and compliance requirements to ensure the best fit. The constraint is to select the most suitable foundation model for a specific business problem, considering multiple criteria. Order the following steps to evaluate and select a foundation model for a new generative AI application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Conduct extensive testing and benchmarking of shortlisted models with representative code generation tasks and data.","Identify the specific requirements for the code generation feature, including programming languages, complexity, and desired output quality.","Evaluate models for compliance with industry regulations and internal security policies.","Review available foundation models on Amazon Bedrock, comparing their stated capabilities, context window sizes, and pricing structures."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}'::int[],
  'The correct sequence for evaluating and selecting a foundation model begins with understanding the specific needs. Option B (Identify the specific requirements for the code generation feature, including programming languages, complexity, and desired output quality) defines the criteria. Option D (Review available foundation models on Amazon Bedrock, comparing their stated capabilities, context window sizes, and pricing structures) involves initial screening of available options. Option A (Conduct extensive testing and benchmarking of shortlisted models with representative code generation tasks and data) is crucial for practical validation. Finally, Option C (Evaluate models for compliance with industry regulations and internal security policies) is a critical non-functional requirement that must be met before final selection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_79',
  'A digital media company wants to explore various generative AI use cases to automate and enhance its content production pipeline, including text, image, and video. They need a structured approach to identify, prioritize, and implement these capabilities across different departments. The constraint is to identify and prioritize generative AI use cases for diverse content creation. Order the following steps to effectively integrate generative AI into their content production.',
  'ordering',
  '["Pilot the selected use cases with a small team, gathering feedback and refining the implementation.","Identify specific content creation bottlenecks and opportunities for automation or enhancement within each department.","Evaluate potential generative AI models and services (e.g., text-to-image, text generation) that align with the identified use cases.","Prioritize use cases based on potential business impact, implementation complexity, and available resources."]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}'::int[],
  'The correct sequence for identifying and implementing generative AI use cases for content creation starts with internal analysis. Option B (Identify specific content creation bottlenecks and opportunities for automation or enhancement within each department) pinpoints where AI can add value. Option D (Prioritize use cases based on potential business impact, implementation complexity, and available resources) ensures efforts are focused on the most impactful and feasible projects. Option C (Evaluate potential generative AI models and services (e.g., text-to-image, text generation) that align with the identified use cases) involves selecting the right tools. Finally, Option A (Pilot the selected use cases with a small team, gathering feedback and refining the implementation) allows for iterative development and validation before broader rollout.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_80',
  'Order the benefits of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) in an enterprise context, from foundational advantages to advanced capabilities.',
  'ordering',
  '["Reduces the likelihood of hallucinations by grounding responses in factual data.","Enables LLMs to access and utilize proprietary, up-to-date company-specific knowledge.","Eliminates the need for costly and time-consuming fine-tuning for knowledge updates.","Provides transparency and explainability by citing sources for generated information."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'A foundational benefit of RAG is its ability to access specific data. Option B (Enables LLMs to access and utilize proprietary, up-to-date company-specific knowledge) is key for enterprise use. This access directly leads to improved accuracy, so Option A (Reduces the likelihood of hallucinations by grounding responses in factual data) follows. A significant practical advantage is avoiding extensive retraining, making Option C (Eliminates the need for costly and time-consuming fine-tuning for knowledge updates) a strong benefit. Finally, a more advanced capability that builds trust is Option D (Provides transparency and explainability by citing sources for generated information).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_81',
  'Order the methods for customizing an LLM''s behavior and knowledge from least complex/costly to most complex/costly.',
  'ordering',
  '["Fine-tuning a foundation model with a custom dataset.","Using Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with external knowledge bases.","Crafting specific instructions and examples within the prompt (prompt engineering).","Training a new foundation model from scratch."]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}'::int[],
  'Customizing an LLM''s behavior starts with the simplest and least costly methods. Option C (Crafting specific instructions and examples within the prompt (prompt engineering)) is the most straightforward. Next, integrating external knowledge without altering the model itself is more involved but still efficient, as described in Option B (Using Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with external knowledge bases). When more significant behavioral changes or domain adaptation is needed, Option A (Fine-tuning a foundation model with a custom dataset) is the next level of complexity and cost. The most complex and expensive method is Option D (Training a new foundation model from scratch), which is typically reserved for highly specialized research or unique requirements.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_82',
  'A company wants to build an AI-powered customer service chatbot that can answer questions based on its proprietary product manuals. Order the architectural components for this solution using AWS services, starting from data ingestion.',
  'ordering',
  '["Use Amazon Bedrock to host a foundation model for generating responses.","Store product manuals in an Amazon S3 bucket.","Create an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base to index the manuals and generate embeddings.","Develop a front-end application to interact with the Amazon Bedrock Agent."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}'::int[],
  'Building a RAG-based chatbot starts with the data source. Option B (Store product manuals in an Amazon S3 bucket) is the initial step for data ingestion. Next, this data needs to be processed for retrieval, which is handled by Option C (Create an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base to index the manuals and generate embeddings). The core of the generative AI is the LLM, so Option A (Use Amazon Bedrock to host a foundation model for generating responses) is the next component. Finally, users need an interface to interact with the system, making Option D (Develop a front-end application to interact with the Amazon Bedrock Agent) the last step.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_83',
  'Order the typical steps in an agentic AI workflow using tools, from receiving a user request to providing a final response.',
  'ordering',
  '["The agent executes the selected tool with the determined parameters.","The agent receives a user request or goal.","The agent analyzes the tool''s output and determines the next action or final response.","The agent plans a sequence of actions, selecting appropriate tools and parameters."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}'::int[],
  'An agentic AI workflow begins with understanding the user''s need. Option B (The agent receives a user request or goal) is the starting point. Based on this, the agent needs to strategize, so Option D (The agent plans a sequence of actions, selecting appropriate tools and parameters) follows. Once a plan is made, the agent acts, making Option A (The agent executes the selected tool with the determined parameters) the next step. Finally, the agent must process the results of its actions to continue or conclude, as in Option C (The agent analyzes the tool''s output and determines the next action or final response).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_84',
  'A business needs to select a generative AI model for a new application that generates marketing copy. Order the considerations for selecting the most appropriate model, from initial business needs to technical evaluation.',
  'ordering',
  '["Evaluate the model''s output quality, coherence, and relevance for marketing copy generation.","Identify the specific business problem and desired outcomes for the marketing application.","Consider the model''s cost, inference speed, and ease of integration with existing systems.","Assess the model''s capabilities, such as text generation, length limits, and language support."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}'::int[],
  'Model selection always starts with understanding the problem. Option B (Identify the specific business problem and desired outcomes for the marketing application) is the foundational step. Once the problem is clear, the model''s inherent abilities are considered, so Option D (Assess the model''s capabilities, such as text generation, length limits, and language support) follows. After confirming capabilities, the quality of the output is paramount, making Option A (Evaluate the model''s output quality, coherence, and relevance for marketing copy generation) the next crucial step. Finally, practical deployment factors come into play, as in Option C (Consider the model''s cost, inference speed, and ease of integration with existing systems).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_85',
  'Order the common limitations of generative AI models from most fundamental (inherent to their design) to most practical (operational challenges).',
  'ordering',
  '["The cost associated with inference, especially for high-volume or complex requests.","The inability to access real-time or proprietary information beyond their training data (knowledge cutoff).","The tendency to generate plausible but factually incorrect information (hallucinations).","Finite input length, limiting the amount of context they can process in a single prompt (context limits)."]'::json,
  '{2,1,3,0}'::int[],
  'Hallucinations are a fundamental challenge for LLMs. Option C (The tendency to generate plausible but factually incorrect information (hallucinations)) is an inherent limitation. Closely related is the knowledge cutoff, as in Option B (The inability to access real-time or proprietary information beyond their training data (knowledge cutoff)), which is a design aspect of their training. Context limits are a practical constraint on how much information can be processed at once, making Option D (Finite input length, limiting the amount of context they can process in a single prompt (context limits)) the next. Finally, operational cost is a significant practical challenge for deploying generative AI, represented by Option A (The cost associated with inference, especially for high-volume or complex requests).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

