INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_69',
  'A company is considering different methods to customize a foundation model. Order these customization approaches from generally LEAST to MOST expensive in terms of training/compute cost.',
  'ordering',
  '["Continued pre-training on a large domain-specific corpus.","Implementing Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG).","Fine-tuning the model on a custom dataset.","Using in-context learning (few-shot prompting)."]'::json,
  '{3,1,2,0}'::int[],
  'In-context learning (Option D) is generally the least expensive as it only involves prompt engineering at inference time. Implementing RAG (Option B) involves managing a knowledge base and embeddings, which is more costly than just prompting but less than retraining. Fine-tuning (Option C) requires training a model on a custom dataset, incurring significant compute costs. Continued pre-training (Option A) is the most expensive as it involves training a model from scratch or near-scratch on a large corpus.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_70',
  'Order the typical steps for setting up a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) system with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the Knowledge Base with a vector store and data source","Create an Amazon Bedrock Agent to orchestrate the RAG process","Upload and sync enterprise data to a supported data source","Invoke the Agent with a user query to retrieve and generate a response"]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}'::int[],
  'First, upload and sync enterprise data (Option C) to a supported data source like Amazon S3. Next, configure the Knowledge Base (Option A) in Amazon Bedrock, linking it to the data source and a vector store. Then, create an Amazon Bedrock Agent (Option B) to define the RAG workflow. Finally, invoke the Agent (Option D) with a user query, which will retrieve relevant information from the Knowledge Base and use it to generate a grounded response.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_71',
  'Order the model selection criteria a developer should consider when choosing a foundation model for a new application, from most fundamental to more advanced considerations.',
  'ordering',
  '["Modality support (text, image, speech)","Latency and throughput requirements","Core capability for the task (e.g., summarization, generation)","Cost per token and overall budget"]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}'::int[],
  'The most fundamental criterion is the Core capability (Option C) of the model to perform the required task. Next, consider Modality support (Option A) to ensure the model handles the necessary input/output types. Latency and throughput requirements (Option B) are critical for user experience and system performance. Finally, Cost per token and overall budget (Option D) are essential for economic viability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_72',
  'Arrange the following foundation model customization approaches from requiring the highest to the lowest cost and effort.',
  'ordering',
  '["Fine-tuning a pre-trained model on a custom dataset","Continued pre-training on a large domain-specific corpus","In-context learning (prompt engineering) with a few examples","Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) with a knowledge base"]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}'::int[],
  'Continued pre-training (Option B) is the most expensive, requiring vast datasets and significant compute. Fine-tuning (Option A) is less costly than pre-training but still requires substantial data and compute. Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) (Option D) involves managing a knowledge base and embeddings, which is generally more complex and costly than prompt engineering alone. In-context learning (Option C), primarily through prompt engineering, is typically the least costly and lowest effort customization method.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_73',
  'Order the process of an Amazon Bedrock Agent executing a multi-step task for a user, from initial request to final response.',
  'ordering',
  '["Agent determines the next action based on its instructions and tools","Agent invokes a tool (e.g., API, Knowledge Base) to gather information","User submits a request to the Amazon Bedrock Agent","Agent generates a final response to the user"]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}'::int[],
  'The process begins when a User submits a request (Option C) to the Amazon Bedrock Agent. The Agent then determines the next action (Option A) by analyzing the request against its defined instructions and available tools. If needed, the Agent invokes a tool (Option B) to perform an action or gather information. Once all necessary steps are completed, the Agent generates a final response (Option D) to the user.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_74',
  'A data scientist is evaluating inference parameters for a generative model to control its output. Order these parameters by their typical impact on output creativity, from least to most creative.',
  'ordering',
  '["Top-K sampling","Temperature","Top-P sampling","Max tokens"]'::json,
  '{3,0,2,1}'::int[],
  'Max tokens (Option D) primarily controls output length, not creativity directly. Top-K sampling (Option A) restricts the model to choose from the K most probable tokens, limiting creativity. Top-P sampling (Option C) selects from the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability exceeds P, offering more flexibility than Top-K. Temperature (Option B) directly influences the randomness of the output, with higher values leading to more creative and diverse responses, making it the most impactful for creativity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_75',
  'Order the steps involved in querying a RAG system to get an answer, starting from the user''s input.',
  'ordering',
  '["Retrieve relevant documents or passages from the knowledge base","Generate an answer using the foundation model and retrieved context","Embed the user''s query into a vector representation","Search the vector store for similar embeddings"]'::json,
  '{2,3,0,1}'::int[],
  'The process begins by embedding the user''s query (Option C) into a vector representation. This vector is then used to search the vector store (Option D) for similar embeddings, identifying relevant information. Based on the search, relevant documents or passages (Option A) are retrieved from the knowledge base. Finally, the foundation model generates an answer (Option B) by synthesizing the user''s query and the retrieved context.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_76',
  'Order the typical data flow when using a vector store with a foundation model for semantic search, from data ingestion to query response.',
  'ordering',
  '["User query is embedded into a vector","Source documents are chunked and embedded into vectors","Similarity search is performed in the vector store","Embedded documents are stored in the vector store"]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}'::int[],
  'The process starts with source documents being chunked and embedded into vectors (Option B). These embedded documents are then stored in the vector store (Option D). When a user query comes in, the user query is embedded into a vector (Option A). Finally, a similarity search is performed in the vector store (Option C) to find the most relevant document chunks based on the query vector.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_77',
  'A startup is developing a new customer service chatbot using Amazon Bedrock. They need to select a foundation model that balances response quality, latency, and cost for real-time interactions. Order the following considerations when making this model selection.',
  'ordering',
  '["Evaluate the model''s performance on a representative dataset for the specific use case.","Compare the per-token cost and inference latency of various suitable models.","Identify the core capabilities (e.g., text generation, summarization) required for the chatbot.","Consider the context window size and modality support relevant to the interaction patterns."]'::json,
  '{2,3,1,0}'::int[],
  'The first step in model selection is to clearly identify the core capabilities that the chatbot requires, such as text generation or summarization (Option C). Once capabilities are aligned, considering the context window size and modality support ensures the model can handle the expected interaction complexity and input types (Option D). With a narrowed down list of capable models, comparing their per-token cost and inference latency becomes critical for real-time, cost-sensitive applications (Option B). Finally, to validate the choice, the model''s performance should be evaluated on a representative dataset specific to the chatbot''s use case (Option A).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_78',
  'A content generation platform wants to use Amazon Bedrock to create diverse marketing copy. They need to adjust model inference parameters to control the creativity and randomness of the output. Order the steps to achieve this.',
  'ordering',
  '["Set a lower ''top_p'' value to narrow down the token sampling pool.","Adjust the ''temperature'' parameter to control the randomness of the output.","Invoke the Amazon Bedrock model with the prompt and adjusted parameters.","Evaluate the generated marketing copy for desired creativity and coherence."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'To control creativity and randomness, the first parameter to adjust is ''temperature'', which directly influences the randomness of token selection (Option B). For more focused and less diverse outputs, a lower ''top_p'' value can be set to narrow the token sampling pool to only the most probable tokens (Option A). Once these parameters are configured, the Amazon Bedrock model is invoked with the prompt and the adjusted inference parameters (Option C). Finally, the generated marketing copy is evaluated to ensure it meets the desired levels of creativity and coherence (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_79',
  'A financial institution is building a secure internal Q&A system using Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases to answer questions based on proprietary compliance documents. Order the steps to set up this Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) solution.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base and configure its settings.","Upload the compliance documents to an Amazon S3 bucket.","Define the data source for the Knowledge Base, linking it to the S3 bucket.","Initiate the data ingestion process for the Knowledge Base."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'The first step in setting up a RAG solution with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases is to prepare the data by uploading the compliance documents to an Amazon S3 bucket (Option B). Next, an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base is created and configured, which will manage the retrieval process (Option A). Following this, the S3 bucket containing the documents is defined as the data source for the Knowledge Base (Option C). Finally, the data ingestion process is initiated, which will chunk the documents, create embeddings, and store them in the associated vector store (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_80',
  'A company is designing an application that requires a foundation model to perform a sequence of complex, multi-step tasks, such as booking a flight or processing a customer order. Order the steps to leverage Amazon Bedrock Agents for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the actions (APIs) the agent can perform and their schemas.","Create an Amazon Bedrock Agent and configure its instructions and capabilities.","Test the agent''s ability to orchestrate tasks and interact with external systems.","Associate the defined actions with the Amazon Bedrock Agent."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}'::int[],
  'To leverage Amazon Bedrock Agents for multi-step tasks, the initial step is to create the Agent itself and provide its high-level instructions and desired capabilities (Option B). Following this, the specific actions (e.g., API calls to external systems) that the agent needs to perform are defined, along with their input/output schemas (Option A). These defined actions are then associated with the Amazon Bedrock Agent, allowing it to understand and utilize them (Option D). Finally, thorough testing of the agent''s orchestration capabilities and its interactions with external systems is crucial to ensure correct behavior (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_81',
  'A development team is deciding between prompt engineering, Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG), and fine-tuning for customizing an Amazon Bedrock foundation model to a specific domain. Order these customization approaches from generally LEAST to MOST expensive and complex to implement.',
  'ordering',
  '["Fine-tuning a foundation model with a custom dataset.","Implementing Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases.","Applying advanced prompt engineering techniques (e.g., few-shot, Chain-of-Thought).","Continued pre-training on a massive domain-specific dataset."]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}'::int[],
  'The least expensive and complex approach is typically applying advanced prompt engineering techniques, as it only involves crafting better prompts without modifying the model or adding external data stores (Option C). Next in complexity and cost is implementing Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases, which involves setting up data sources and vector stores but doesn''t retrain the model (Option B). Fine-tuning a foundation model with a custom dataset is more expensive and complex, as it involves training a new version of the model (Option A). The most expensive and complex approach is continued pre-training on a massive domain-specific dataset, which is akin to training a model from scratch on new data (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_82',
  'A company is developing an application using Amazon Bedrock and needs to select a foundation model. They have identified several candidate models and want to compare them based on key criteria. Order the following criteria by their typical impact on application performance and cost, from most direct to least direct.',
  'ordering',
  '["Context window size and its implications for long inputs.","Model''s core capability (e.g., text generation, image generation, code generation).","Inference latency and per-token cost for API calls.","Multilingual support and its relevance to target user base."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}'::int[],
  'The most direct impact on application performance and cost comes from inference latency and per-token cost, as these directly translate to response times and operational expenses for every API call (Option C). Following this, the context window size directly impacts the length of input the model can process, which can affect the need for chunking or more complex RAG strategies, influencing performance and cost (Option A). Multilingual support is important for reaching a broad user base and can influence the complexity of localization, affecting overall project cost and reach, but less directly than latency or token cost (Option D). The model''s core capability is a foundational selection criterion, but its direct impact on performance and cost is less granular than the other options, as it defines what the model *can* do, rather than *how efficiently* it does it (Option B).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_83',
  'A company wants to build an AI agent using Amazon Bedrock to automate multi-step customer support tasks, such as looking up order status and updating customer profiles. Order the initial steps for setting up such an agent.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the actions the agent can perform by providing API schemas and descriptions.","Create an Amazon Bedrock Agent and configure its instructions and foundation model.","Provide a knowledge base (e.g., Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases) if the agent needs to retrieve information.","Test the agent''s ability to execute multi-step tasks and refine its instructions or actions."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'Setting up an Amazon Bedrock Agent begins by creating the agent itself and configuring its instructions and the foundation model it will use (Option B). Next, define the actions the agent can perform by providing API schemas and descriptions, allowing it to interact with external systems (Option A). If the agent needs to retrieve information from internal documents, provide a knowledge base, such as Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases (Option C). Finally, test the agent''s ability to execute multi-step tasks and refine its instructions or actions based on the observed behavior (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_84',
  'A development team is planning to integrate a foundation model into a new application. They need to select the most appropriate model from Amazon Bedrock. Order the key criteria they should consider during the model selection process.',
  'ordering',
  '["Evaluate the model''s performance on representative tasks and data.","Assess the model''s cost per token and overall inference latency.","Determine the required capabilities, such as text generation, summarization, or image understanding.","Consider the model''s context window size and supported modalities."]'::json,
  '{2,3,1,0}'::int[],
  'When selecting a foundation model, the first step is to determine the required capabilities, such as text generation, summarization, or image understanding, to ensure the model can perform the task (Option C). Next, consider the model''s context window size and supported modalities (e.g., text, image) to match application needs (Option D). Then, assess the model''s cost per token and overall inference latency, which are critical for operational efficiency and user experience (Option B). Finally, evaluate the model''s performance on representative tasks and data to confirm it meets accuracy and quality requirements (Option A). Always choosing the largest, most powerful model for every task is an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_85',
  'A company wants to enable its Amazon Bedrock foundation model to answer questions using proprietary company documents. Order the typical steps for setting up Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base and configure a data source (e.g., Amazon S3).","Connect the Knowledge Base to an Amazon Bedrock Agent or directly to your application.","Ingest documents into the Knowledge Base, which automatically creates vector embeddings.","Configure the vector store (e.g., Amazon OpenSearch Service) to store and search embeddings."]'::json,
  '{0,3,2,1}'::int[],
  'To set up RAG with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases, first create an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base and configure a data source, such as an Amazon S3 bucket where your documents are stored (Option A). Next, configure the vector store, like Amazon OpenSearch Service, which will be used to store and search the vector embeddings of your documents (Option D). Then, ingest the documents into the Knowledge Base; this process automatically creates and stores vector embeddings (Option C). Finally, connect the Knowledge Base to an Amazon Bedrock Agent or directly to your application to enable RAG functionality (Option B).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_1_86',
  'A developer is implementing a semantic search feature using a vector database. Order the common steps involved in processing a user query to retrieve relevant documents.',
  'ordering',
  '["Perform a vector similarity search in the vector database.","Convert the user''s natural language query into a vector embedding.","Retrieve the original documents or metadata associated with the top similar vectors.","Send the retrieved documents along with the original query to a foundation model for generation."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'The process begins by converting the user''s natural language query into a vector embedding using an embedding model (Option B). This embedding is then used to perform a vector similarity search in the vector database, identifying documents with similar semantic meaning (Option A). Next, retrieve the original documents or metadata associated with the top similar vectors found in the search (Option C). Finally, for RAG applications, send the retrieved documents along with the original query to a foundation model for generating a contextually relevant response (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

